        <div id="hot-vs-iced" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Clumps in Hot Coffee (and Not in Iced)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coffee is typically brewed with water at about 90 to 96 degrees Celsius, roughly 195 to 205 degrees Fahrenheit, and a fresh cup is often served well above 70 degrees Celsius. The main whey proteins begin to unfold, or denature, at around that temperature. When you tip a scoop of whey into a hot mug, the outside of each clump of powder heats instantly, its proteins unfold and stick together, and they form a skin that stops liquid reaching the dry powder inside. The result is the floating, rubbery lumps that most protein coffee complaints describe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold liquid avoids the problem entirely. In iced coffee or cold brew, whey disperses the same way it does in a shaker bottle, and an isolate dissolves particularly cleanly because it has less fat and lactose than concentrate. That is why the simplest fix for protein coffee is often to drink it iced, or to let a hot cup cool for a few minutes before adding powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen behaves differently. Collagen peptides are made by breaking collagen down into short fragments, the same family of protein as gelatin, and they dissolve readily in hot liquid. Hot coffee does not make them clump, which is why collagen has become the default powder for people who want protein in a steaming mug. The catch is nutritional rather than practical, and we cover it in the collagen section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Denaturing sounds alarming, but it does not destroy protein's nutritional value. Cooking an egg or grilling a steak denatures protein too, and your stomach acid unfolds proteins anyway as part of normal digestion. Heat-denatured whey still supplies the same amino acids. The problem with whey in hot coffee is texture, not nutrition, so if you can live with a few specks, or you mix it properly, there is no reason to avoid it.
          </p>

